@meta
id: 27-04
title: Drill — flip it with a bang
chapter: 27
type: drill
skills: set-option
par: 28
minutes: 4
reps: 8
vscode: nothing — there is no toggle, only a settings page
open: query.sql

@brief
Eight times: turn `expandtab` off with a toggle rather than by typing
`noexpandtab`, then indent the `WHERE` line so it gets one real tab
character. Save, quit, go again.

The point is the `!`. Appending it to a boolean option flips whatever the
option currently is, which is what you want on a key you press all day —
`:set list!`, `:set wrap!`, `:set spell!`.

@keys
| `:set expandtab!` | flip the option to the other thing |
| `:set invexpandtab` | the same, spelled out |
| `:set expandtab?` | check which way it ended up |

@hints
`!` goes on the end of the option name, with no space.
--
`:set expandtab!` then Enter, then `2G>>`.

@solution
    :set expandtab!<Enter>  2G  >>  :wq<Enter>

Twenty-four keystrokes.

`:set opt!` is the form you reach for interactively, and the form you put
on the right-hand side of a mapping. `:set opt` and `:set noopt` are what
you write in a config file, where there is no "current value" to flip and
you want the file to say what it means.

There is a wrinkle worth knowing before it bites you. `!` on a boolean
toggles it. `!` on a *number* or *string* option is not a toggle at all —
`:set shiftwidth!` is simply an error. And `:set opt!` on an option vim
does not have is an error too, which is how you find out you spelled
`expandtab` wrong.

@start query.sql
SELECT id, total_pence FROM orders
WHERE status = 'CANCELLED'

@expected query.sql
SELECT id, total_pence FROM orders
	WHERE status = 'CANCELLED'
